# CODEOWNERS — fapico2
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DO, read this before assuming it is active.
#
# A CODEOWNERS file is INERT on its own. It assigns an owner to paths; nothing
# enforces anything until a branch RULESET is configured with the
# `require_code_owner_review` rule. Applying the ruleset is a separate,
# manual step — see docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-30-github-ci-security-design.md
# §5.4 and .github/rulesets/main.json. Until that is applied, this file
# documents intent and nothing more.
#
# WHY THESE PATHS
#
# The property that makes this tree trustworthy is that its gates are hard to
# weaken by accident. That property has exactly one weak point: a pull request
# that edits the gate that judges it. Everything below is either a gate, the
# policy a gate enforces, or code whose failure mode is a security failure
# rather than a test failure.
#
# Ordering: later matches win in CODEOWNERS, so the specific paths come after
# the broad .github/ catch-all and correctly override it.

# --- The gates themselves, and the machinery that judges the build ----------
# ci.yml's 13 jobs call these scripts; release.yml's signing path calls four of
# them directly. A PR that relaxes a threshold or deletes a negative control is
# the highest-value edit available to an attacker who can open a PR.
/.github/CODEOWNERS @eddieoz
/.github/workflows/ @eddieoz
/tests/scripts/ @eddieoz
/supply-chain/ @eddieoz

# --- Supply-chain policy -----------------------------------------------------
# deny.toml decides which licences, advisories and duplicate versions are
# refused; deny-graph.toml carries the workspace's own edge assertions. Both
# are policy, and both are enforced by the supply-chain job.
/deny.toml @eddieoz
/deny-graph.toml @eddieoz

# --- Build configuration -----------------------------------------------------
# .cargo/config.toml selects the target and injects rustflags; the ARM linker
# flags are load-bearing for the RP2350 image, and a change here changes what
# bytes ship without changing a line of Rust.
/.cargo/ @eddieoz
/Cargo.toml @eddieoz
/Cargo.lock @eddieoz
/rust-toolchain.toml @eddieoz
/.cargo/config.toml @eddieoz

# --- Secure-boot and key-handling surface -----------------------------------
# boot.rs is where the OTP key row is read and the OTP write-lock is applied
# (see AGENTS.md: the write-lock is runtime-only and does not persist across a
# reset — a known limitation, US-918). uf2gen.py produces the only flashable
# image format the RP2350 bootrom accepts. secrets/ is ignored except its
# README; keeping it listed means a proposal to commit a key is caught here
# rather than by luck.
/firmware/src/boot.rs @eddieoz
/firmware/uf2gen.py @eddieoz
/secrets/ @eddieoz

# --- Security documentation --------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY.md is the vulnerability-reporting policy and the statement of what
# this firmware does and does not guarantee. Editing it changes what users are
# told about their threat model.
/SECURITY.md @eddieoz
/docs/SECURITY-ASSESSMENT-ROUND2.md @eddieoz
/docs/secureboot.md @eddieoz
/docs/supply-chain.md @eddieoz

# NOTE ON SCOPE: `/vendor/` is NOT listed.
#
# The vendored trees (vendor/opcard, vendor/ed448-goldilocks, vendor/x448) are
# AGPL/LGPL code carried from upstream. They are ordinary dependencies from a
# review standpoint — a change there is a change to third-party code, gated by
# cargo-deny/cargo-vet and the SBOM like any other. Requiring owner review on
# them would make every upstream sync a bottleneck without adding a property,
# because a malicious edit there is caught by the same gates that catch a
# malicious edit anywhere else in the dependency graph.
